{0 Testing a worker}

Test your worker with [workerd test] to run the compiled WebAssembly in the
Workers runtime. This checks the routes and bindings in your test setup without
deploying the worker. See {{!page-limits}Limits} for Cloudflare platform limits.

{1 Run a worker test}

+ Install [workerd] on [PATH]. The repository CI uses [npm install -g workerd].
+ Build the worker: [workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js --out _worker-test].
+ Write a [workerd] config. List the files of [_worker-test] as modules
  ([.js] as [esModule], [.wasm] as [wasm]). Bind the worker to a test worker as
  a service.
+ In the test worker, call the service with [env.WORKER.fetch(url)] and compare
  the status, headers, and body. Call
  [env.WORKER.scheduled({ cron, scheduledTime })] to test the cron handler.
+ Run [workerd test config.capnp].

[test/worker/run.sh] shows a working config generator. It builds
[test/worker/main.ml], then runs the suites imported by
[test/worker/tests.mjs]. Set [globalOutbound] to a test worker to answer
{!Workero.fetch} calls without the network. The repository tests exercise
compiled Wasm, JSPI waits, and the [workerd] runtime. They use JS test services
for KV, R2, and Cache. D1 uses a wrapped adapter backed by Durable Object
SQLite. These substitutes test workero calls against the adapter interfaces.
They do not test production Cloudflare storage services.

An exception that escapes a handler fails a service-binding call. An HTTP
request gets status 500 instead.

{1 Test code without the worker runtime}

For code that does not wait, such as parsing with {!Workero.URL}, use the
Alcotest unit tests in [test/unit/]. They compile with [(modes js)] and run in
Node via [dune test]. They do not exercise compiled Wasm or the worker runtime.
Node's JSPI support depends on the version. Use [workerd test] for worker code
that calls {!Workero.Promise.await}.
